---
import Developers from "~/layouts/Developers.astro";
import { SECURITY_MODEL, SECURITY_POLICY } from "~/data/developers";

const servers = [
  [
    "Made up for the purpose",
    "Create as many people as it wants, like any e-mail provider. A site that needs real people needs its own checks.",
  ],
  [
    "Compromised",
    "Sign in as any of its own people. It never holds their passkeys, but it can sign a token without one.",
  ],
  [
    "Run by a hostile operator",
    "The same. Choosing one's server is choosing whom to trust; the project says so to people.",
  ],
];

const guards = [
  ["https only, a domain name", "Never an address literal, never plain http."],
  [
    "Public addresses only",
    "Loopback, private, shared and link-local ranges (the cloud metadata address included) are dropped by the resolver itself, so the address checked is the one connected to: DNS rebinding included.",
  ],
  ["No redirect, no proxy", "A document that moved is not followed."],
  ["5 KiB, 10 seconds", "Larger or slower: refused."],
  [
    "Errors never cached",
    "A valid document is kept 5 minutes to a day, and used while a new read fails.",
  ],
];
---

<Developers
  title="Security and trust · Developers"
  description="What a site can trust of a brig·id server it has never seen, what a server does when it reads a site's document, and how to report a vulnerability."
  lead="Security and trust,"
  accent="stated plainly."
  intro="Accepting people from servers you have never seen is the point of brig·id, and it has limits. Here is what holds, what does not, and what a server does to protect itself."
>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>What a site can trust of a server</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing a site can check proves that a server runs brig·id unmodified, is
      not compromised, or is not a server made to look like one: a server can
      claim any edition and version. What holds is narrower, and it covers the
      main threat:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>
          A token is signed by the key its <code>iss</code> publishes over TLS.
        </strong>{" "}
        Its <code>iss</code> is the server the person named, its
        <code>aud</code> your <code>client_id</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A server speaks only for its own people.</strong> Know a person
        by <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code> together: a false or
        compromised server signs with its own <code>iss</code>, so it can never
        sign in as someone from another server.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What remains</h3>
  </div>
  <table class="ref rise">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">A server that is…</th>
        <th scope="col">Can</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {servers.map(([who, can]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{who}</th>
          <td>{can}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      Lists signed by brig.id (OpenID Federation trust marks: servers whose
      operator is known, sites reported for abuse) are planned for after the
      first version. They will say who answers for a server, never that it is
      honest, and following them will always be a choice.
    </p>

    <h2>What a server does with a site's document</h2>
    <p>
      Reading a document is a request a server makes to an address anyone
      chooses. A brig·id server makes it narrow:
    </p>
  </div>
  <table class="ref rise">
    <tbody>
      {guards.map(([rule, text]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{rule}</th>
          <td>{text}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      The person always sees the site's host first: its declared name only
      beside it, its logo never fetched from it. The operator decides which
      sites are accepted and caps what they may ask for.
    </p>

    <h2>What a site should do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Identify people by <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code>, never by
        e-mail.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check <code>state</code>, <code>iss</code> in the answer, then the ID
        token: signature, issuer, audience, nonce, expiry.
      </li>
      <li>Keep your private key on your server; a page or an app has none.</li>
      <li>
        Ask only for what you need, and say which attributes are optional.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <p>
      Privately, through GitHub's security advisories:
      <a href={SECURITY_POLICY}>the security policy</a> says how and what to
      expect. Please do not open a public issue for a flaw. The full threat
      model is in <a href={SECURITY_MODEL}>the security model</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Developers>
